WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.400
Guys sometimes a play is so epic,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.780
so perfectly timed, so clutch,

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.660
that it lives on forever in League of Legends, as an iconic moment.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.760
Unfortunately the same can also be said for the worst of the worst misplays.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.360
So from bad Baron plays that lost the game,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.240
to the most infamous ult that never happened, here are the Top 10 Misplays in League of Legends.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.320
To  kick off our list of fails we’ve got one

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.260
that’s so well known it’s become a meme.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.360
In what has since become known as “pulling a Dyrus” we have what might be one of the

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.320
funniest images in League history.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.900
As the casters and the camera are focused on the early game mid lane matchup they’re

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.280
interrupted abruptly:

00:00:49.360 --> 00:01:01.620
(Casting)

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.300
He pulled a Dyrus.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.280
He died farming wolves 8 and a half minutes into the game.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.560
The Flash Summoner spell is undoubtedly the

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.820
most important and powerful in League of Legends.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.420
And when a Flash goes wrong, it goes really wrong.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.920
Coming in at number 9 xPeke shows us just how bad a Flash can be.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.980
He blinks over the wall directly in the middle of the team fight and his health bar

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.740
melts on site.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:41.580
(Casting)

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.060
He tried to play Sivir like a midlane mage.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.820
Not a lot of burst damage there.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.940
At number 8 Doublelift shows us that even the best in the world need to ease

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.180
their way back into competitive play sometimes.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.200
After a tough team fight where Team Liquid had their backs against the wall, Doublelift

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.720
and the surviving members of TL looked to regroup and farm-up.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:08.540
Double rotates to the red buff and gets ready for the possibility of another fight.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.220
Little did he know that the real fight was right in front of him.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.060
a he dies embarrassingly to the cold claws of the Red brambleback.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:34.400
(Casting)

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.440
Nothing brings you back to earth quite like being punked out by an NPC in front of tens

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:40.960
of thousands of people.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.480
At number seven we’ve got what can only be described as a swing … and a miss.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:52.120
If you’ve ever played League you’re definitely familiar with just how game breaking a strong

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.220
Annie's ultimate can be.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.340
That is, if you actually can hit your opponents with it..

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:02.560
Unfortunately KiWi Kid was unable to land his ult after flashing forward and unleashed

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.440
Tibbers into … well … nobody.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:35.780
(Casting)

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:40.700
When it comes to throwing games in the North American LCS, perhaps noone is as noteworthy

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:41.800
as Coast.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.000
They’re the champions of giving it all away.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.680
And here, is maybe the greatest example of just that.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.740
Coast held a small lead over Dignitas until their mid laner Jesiz got caught out — to

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.320
which they responded by trying to force Baron.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.939
At number six we've got one of the stranger calls you're ever likely to see, Dig was

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:06.920
able to counter by simply walking their team down the mid lane and destroying Coast’s nexus.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:41.540
(Casting)

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.080
Vision control is arguably the single most

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.480
important thing in League of Legends.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.600
Whichever team has eyes on the majority of the map, is often the one with a lead.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.460
Here we see a hilarious example of this.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:59.640
Samsung all pile into a bush looking to catch Najin off-guard and land a pick.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.280
Little do they know, Najin has vision on that
bush.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:37.040
(Casting)

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:42.140
At number four we’ve got the Flash, or lack there of, heard round the League of Legends

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:43.220
community.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:48.160
We all know that at the centre of any good League meme is usually a hilarious

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:48.660
misplay.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:54.740
And maybe no pro player meme is as ubiquitous as Doublelift’s flash still being up.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.180
Here’s where it all started, at World’s in 2017 his team is wrapped up in a brutal

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:01.840
team fight against Misfits.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.100
And then double dies,

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:06.920
With that damn flash, sitting there simply unused.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:12.780
(Casting)

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.120
"I get f*cked right here and I 100% agree,

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.260
this is a situation where I should have flashed,

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:25.600
100% I don't need to get hit by chain or the flash from Dragus. I didn't react in time."

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:31.740
When you queue into a game of League of Legends, you’re expecting a five versus five match.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:35.380
An even affair that will inevitably determine a winner.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:40.780
But sometimes that opposing turret feels like the 6th-man that you never thought to worry

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:41.640
about.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:46.380
And other times, the opposing teams makes you feel a little bit better by getting double

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.760
killed by the rift herald.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:53.660
At number three we’ve got maybe the funniest clip out there — two NPC’s getting the

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:57.080
better of four pro League of Legends players.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:21.560
(Casting)

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:26.880
The runner-up on our list comes from the 2017 League of Legends World Championship.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.740
North America had high hopes for Immortals at Worlds 2017, but after posting a middling

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:39.040
2-2 group stage record, looked to get back on track against an 0-4 Fnatic squad.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.480
As Fnatic started to turn the tide of a key late game team fight.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.620
Immortals star Cody Sun, tried to make a big play to  clutch it out.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:49.640
But it was more than a little awkward.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:08:02.080
(Casting)

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.520
Of all the misplays on this list, this one may have been the most costly.

00:08:08.529 --> 00:08:14.260
TSM and Cloud9 were facing off in Game 5 of the NA LCS finals and the two rivals were

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:17.280
neck and neck when the game’s pivotal teamfight broke out..

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:22.760
In what is maybe the most memorable ULT that never happened, Jensen just never Chronobreaks

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:27.200
in this team fight and TSM are able to capitalize
and win the title.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:32.320
Taking the top spot on our list is that most impactful ultimate that never happened.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:09:15.460
(Casting)

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:20.800
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

